## Solution

**Constraint analysis.** The feasible set is defined by $x^4 + 36 \le 13x^2$, i.e.
$$x^4 - 13x^2 + 36 \le 0.$$
Setting $y = x^2 \ge 0$, this becomes $y^2 - 13y + 36 \le 0$, which factors as
$$(y-4)(y-9) \le 0.$$
Hence $4 \le y \le 9$, i.e. $4 \le x^2 \le 9$. The feasible set is therefore
$$x \in [-3,-2] \cup [2,3].$$

**Monotonicity of $f$.** We have $f(x) = x^3 - 3x$ and
$$f'(x) = 3x^2 - 3 = 3(x^2 - 1).$$
On both intervals of the feasible set, $x^2 \ge 4 > 1$, so $f'(x) > 0$ throughout. Thus $f$ is **strictly increasing** on each component interval.

**Evaluating at the right endpoints.** Since $f$ is increasing on each interval, the maximum on each is attained at the right endpoint:

- On $[-3,-2]$: $\max = f(-2) = (-2)^3 - 3(-2) = -8 + 6 = -2$.
- On $[2,3]$: $\max = f(3) = 3^3 - 3\cdot 3 = 27 - 9 = 18$.

**Feasibility check.** $x = 3$ satisfies $3^4 + 36 = 81 + 36 = 117 = 13 \cdot 9 = 13 \cdot 3^2$, so equality holds and $x=3$ is feasible.

Comparing $-2$ and $18$, the global maximum is

$$\boxed{18}.$$

